---
import Doc from "../../layouts/Doc.astro";
import MethodologyNav from "../../components/MethodologyNav.astro";
import MethodFacts from "../../components/MethodFacts.astro";
import SubcheckTable from "../../components/SubcheckTable.astro";
import { M, axisByKey, percent } from "../../lib/methodology";

const axis = axisByKey("maintenance");
const b = M.maintenance_bands;
const f = M.forge_bounds;
const last = (xs: number[][]) => xs[xs.length - 1];
const notes = {
  recency: "A commit within the last month.",
  issue_responsiveness: "Outside issues answered by a maintainer within days.",
  release_cadence: "A release in the last month, or a short median gap between releases.",
  bus_factor: "Three or more people committing regularly.",
  repository_signals: "Not scored today: it ranks a server within its category, which needs a distribution across every scanned server — a pass that does not exist yet. A limit of ours.",
};
const signalsShare = percent(M.subcheck_weights.maintenance.find((s) => s.key === "repository_signals")!.weight);
---

<Doc
  title="Maintenance — mcpwatchman methodology"
  description="How mcpwatchman's Maintenance axis scores MCP servers: commit recency, release cadence, issue responsiveness and bus factor, with every band and the limits of a bounded fetch."
  canonical="https://mcpwatchman.com/methodology/maintenance/"
  heading="Maintenance"
  crumb={{ href: "/methodology/", label: "Methodology" }}
>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    <p>
      The likelihood that a problem found tomorrow gets fixed. An unmaintained
      server with no findings today is a riskier choice than a maintained one
      with a medium finding, because the first cannot answer the next one.
      Provisional weight {percent(axis.weight)}%.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
  <MethodFacts slot="facts" />

  <MethodologyNav />

  <section class="paper doc-paper" aria-labelledby="sub-heading">
    <h2 id="sub-heading">Sub-checks</h2>
    <p>
      Signals are read from the declared repository through the GitHub API.
      A repository on another forge, or one that cannot be read, leaves the
      axis unassessed. Because repository signals are never scored today, the
      axis is measured on at most {100 - Number(signalsShare)}% of itself.
    </p>
    <SubcheckTable axis="maintenance" caption="Maintenance sub-checks" notes={notes} />

    <h3>Recency of activity</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col" class="num">Score</th><th scope="col">Last commit</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {b.recency_days.map(([days, score]) => (
            <tr><td class="num">{score}</td><td>within {days} days</td></tr>
          ))}
          <tr><td class="num">0</td><td>more than {last(b.recency_days)[0]} days ago</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A stable server is not penalised for being stable: one commit a month
      on a finished project is maintained.
    </p>

    <h3>Release cadence</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col" class="num">Score</th><th scope="col">Releases over the last year</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="num">{b.release_gap_days[0][1]}</td><td>released in the last {b.recent_release_days} days, or a median gap of at most {b.release_gap_days[0][0]} days</td></tr>
          {b.release_gap_days.slice(1).map(([days, score]) => (
            <tr><td class="num">{score}</td><td>median gap of at most {days} days</td></tr>
          ))}
          <tr><td class="num">30</td><td>released in the last year, but a median gap over {last(b.release_gap_days)[0]} days</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">0</td><td>no release in the last year</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Issue responsiveness</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col" class="num">Score</th><th scope="col">Median time to a maintainer's first response</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {b.issue_response_days.map(([days, score]) => (
            <tr><td class="num">{score}</td><td>at most {days} days</td></tr>
          ))}
          <tr><td class="num">0</td><td>over {last(b.issue_response_days)[0]} days, or no response on any issue</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Issues from the last six months count. A repository with fewer than{" "}
      {b.issue_sample_min} of them uses its lifetime median instead. Issues
      the project filed itself — a maintainer's tracking issue, a bot's
      notice — are left out: the question is whether someone outside will be
      answered.
    </p>

    <h3>Bus factor</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col" class="num">Score</th><th scope="col">People with at least {b.bus_factor_min_commits} commits in the last year</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="num">100</td><td>three or more</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">80</td><td>two</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">50</td><td>one</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">30</td><td>one, with no activity in the last {b.bus_factor_stale_days} days</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Sole maintainers are not penalised harshly; many of the best servers in
      the registry have one. Release and dependency bots are excluded by name.
      An AI agent committing under its own identity counts as an author, as a
      person does, so a sole maintainer working through an agent can read as
      two.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="bounded-heading">
    <h2 id="bounded-heading">A bounded fetch never asserts an absence</h2>
    <p>
      The scanner reads a bounded sample of each repository: up to{" "}
      {f.issue_sample_size} issues, {f.tag_sample_size} tags and{" "}
      {f.max_commit_pages} pages of {f.commit_page_size} commits. “We found
      none” in a sample is not “there are none”. When the unread part of the
      history could change a band — a release tag beyond the first{" "}
      {f.tag_sample_size}, an author whose commits continue past the last page
      — the sub-check abstains, and the page names our bound as the reason
      rather than blaming the publisher.
    </p>
  </section>
</Doc>
